    <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
    <Nav />
    <Breadcrumb
        items={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Guides", href: "/guides" },
            { label: "Loguru for Python" },
        ]}
    />

    <main>
        <header class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border pt-[19px]">
            <div
                class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"
                aria-hidden="true"
                style="background-image: linear-gradient(to right, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(to bottom, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 60px 60px; mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 78% 68% at 22% 26%, black, transparent);"
            >
            </div>
            <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-16 pt-20 md:pb-20 md:pt-28">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-muted">
                    <span class="border border-primary/40 px-2 py-1 text-primary">Logging</span>
                    <span>10 min read</span>
                </div>
                <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-[18ch] font-display text-[clamp(40px,6.3vw,72px)] font-medium leading-[0.98] tracking-[-0.045em] text-fg">
                    Loguru for Python
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-7 max-w-[54ch] font-display text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted md:text-lg">
                    Set up Loguru in an application, then send its logs to OpenTelemetry with the trace ID of the request that wrote them.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                    <a href={authorUrl} rel="noopener" class="flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors hover:text-fg">
                        <img src={author.avatar} alt="" width="20" height="20" class="h-5 w-5 rounded-full ring-1 ring-border" />
                        <span>By {author.name}</span>
                    </a>
                    <time datetime="2026-10-20">Updated {updatedLabel}</time>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="border-b border-border bg-bg-deep">
            <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-6 overflow-x-auto px-6 py-4 font-mono text-[10px] whitespace-nowrap text-fg-muted">
                {sectionNav.map((h) => (
                    <a href={`#${h.slug}`} class="transition-colors hover:text-primary">{h.text}</a>
                ))}
            </div>
        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={toc} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="loguru-guide min-w-0">
                <section id="what-is-loguru" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>01</span> · Definition</p>
                    <h2>What Loguru is</h2>

                    <div class="guide-answer">
                        <p><strong>Loguru</strong> is a logging library for Python. You import one ready-made <code>logger</code> and call it. Where the lines go, which levels pass and how they look is set with one method, <code>logger.add()</code>.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        Loguru is free and MIT-licensed, and the current release is 0.7.3. It sits next to the standard <code>logging</code> module rather than replacing it: your dependencies keep logging through <code>logging</code>, and <a href="#intercept">an intercept handler</a> sends their records into Loguru. Each system on this page has its own color:
                    </p>
                    <p class="sys-legend">
                        {Object.values(systems).map((s) => <span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${s.color}`}>{s.name}</span>)}
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">pip install loguru</h3>
                    <p class="cmd-line"><span>Install</span><code>python -m pip install loguru</code></p>
                    <p>
                        <code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'loguru'</code> means the package went into a different interpreter than the one running your code, usually another virtual environment. <code>python -m pip</code> installs into the interpreter you name, so use the same <code>python</code> that starts the app.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="first-line" class="scroll-mt-24">from loguru import logger</h3>
                    <p>
                        The imported <code>logger</code> already has one destination, colored lines on <code>sys.stderr</code> for <code>DEBUG</code> and above. There is no <code>getLogger(__name__)</code> step: Loguru reads the calling module from the stack.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(quickstartCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(quickstartOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>

                    <div class="anatomy" role="img" aria-label="The parts of a default Loguru line: the time comes from {time}, INFO from {level}, __main__:<module>:4 from {name}:{function}:{line}, and the text from {message}.">
                        <div class="anatomy__line" aria-hidden="true">
                            {anatomy.map(([text, field, body], index) => (
                                <>
                                    {index > 0 && <span class="anatomy__sep">{index === 3 ? "-" : "|"}</span>}
                                    <div class="anatomy__col">
                                        <span class="anatomy__part">{text}</span>
                                        <div class="anatomy__key"><code>{field}</code><span>{body}</span></div>
                                    </div>
                                </>
                            ))}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Each part is a field of the log <strong>record</strong>, the dict Loguru builds for every call. The same field names drive <a href="#format">custom formats</a>, <a href="#json">JSON output</a> and the <a href="#bridge">OpenTelemetry bridge</a>. Messages use <code>str.format</code> placeholders, <code>{"{}"}</code> and <code>{"{percent}"}</code>, not <code>%s</code>. Keyword arguments fill named placeholders and are also stored in the record's <code>extra</code> dict.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="configure" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>02</span> · Configure</p>
                    <h2>Levels, sinks and files</h2>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>sink</strong> is a destination for log lines: a stream such as <code>sys.stderr</code>, a file path, a function or a coroutine. Each <code>logger.add()</code> call adds one sink with its own level, format and filter, and returns an integer ID that <code>logger.remove(id)</code> takes. The default sink is <code>sys.stderr</code> at <code>DEBUG</code>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="set-level" class="scroll-mt-24">Set the log level</h3>
                    <p>A sink's level is fixed when it is added, so replace the default sink with your own:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(setLevelCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.remove()</code> without an ID removes every sink. To change the default sink's level without editing code, start the process with <code>LOGURU_LEVEL=INFO</code>. A dict <code>filter</code> sets a minimum per module instead, here <code>INFO</code> overall and <code>WARNING</code> for <code>botocore</code>:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(filterCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="levels" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru levels</h3>
                    <p>
                        Loguru keeps the standard library's level numbers and adds <code>TRACE</code> and <code>SUCCESS</code>. The last column is the severity a record gets once it reaches OpenTelemetry through the <a href="#bridge">bridge sink</a>:
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Level</th>
                                    <th>No.</th>
                                    <th>Method</th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.stdlib.color}`}>logging</span></th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.otel.color}`}>OpenTelemetry</span></th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {levels.map((l) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class={`level level--${l.name.toLowerCase()}`}>{l.name}</span></td>
                                        <td>{l.no}</td>
                                        <td><code>{l.method}</code></td>
                                        <td>{l.stdlib === "None" ? <span class="muted">None</span> : <code>{l.stdlib}</code>}</td>
                                        <td><code>{l.otel}</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A level on a sink is a minimum: <code>level="INFO"</code> passes <code>INFO</code>, <code>SUCCESS</code> and everything above. Register a custom level with a number, then log to it by name:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(customLevelCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The number decides where a custom level sorts and which OpenTelemetry severity it gets: 35 exports as <code>WARN4</code>. The <a href="/guides/python-logging-levels">Python logging levels guide</a> covers which level to pick for a message.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="files" class="scroll-mt-24">Rotate, retain and compress files</h3>
                    <p>Pass a path to <code>logger.add()</code> and the sink writes a file. Rotation, retention and compression are arguments on the same call:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(fileCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Argument</th><th>Accepts</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>rotation</code></td><td>A size, a time of day, an interval or a weekday</td><td><code>"100 MB"</code>, <code>"00:00"</code>, <code>"1 week"</code>, <code>"monday at 12:00"</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>retention</code></td><td>A maximum age, or the number of rotated files to keep</td><td><code>"14 days"</code>, <code>10</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>compression</code></td><td>An archive format for each rotated file</td><td><code>"gz"</code>, <code>"zip"</code>, <code>"tar.gz"</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        On rotation, Loguru renames the current file with a timestamp, for example <code>app.2026-10-20_18-36-50_867066.log.gz</code>, and starts a new <code>app.log</code>. Retention only counts files that match the sink's own name, so <code>app.log</code> and <code>errors.log</code> clean up independently.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="format" class="scroll-mt-24">Change the format</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>format</code> is a template over the record fields from the default line. Tags such as <code>&lt;green&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;level&gt;</code> add color, and Loguru strips them when the sink is a file or a stream that is not a terminal.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(formatCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(formatOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>{"{time:YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss.SSS}"}</code> formats the timestamp, and <code>{"{extra[request_id]}"}</code> reads one key from <code>extra</code>. Other fields include <code>module</code>, <code>function</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>thread</code> and <code>elapsed</code>.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="structured" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Structure</p>
                    <h2>JSON logs and context</h2>

                    <h3 id="json" class="scroll-mt-24">JSON output with serialize=True</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>serialize=True</code> writes each record as one JSON line holding the formatted <code>text</code> and the whole <code>record</code> (trimmed here):
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(serializeCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(serializeOutput, "json")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Log pipelines usually want a flat object with context at the top level. A function sink builds exactly that: Loguru calls it with each formatted message, and <code>message.record</code> is the same record dict.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(jsonSinkCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(jsonSinkOutput, "json")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="context" class="scroll-mt-24">bind() and contextualize()</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.bind()</code> returns a new logger that adds fixed values to <code>extra</code> on every record. The global <code>logger</code> is unchanged, so pass the bound logger to the code that should carry the values:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(bindCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.contextualize()</code> sets values for everything logged inside a <code>with</code> block, including functions that use the plain global <code>logger</code>. It stores them in a <code>contextvars</code> variable, so each asyncio task and thread sees its own. Set a request ID this way once, in middleware:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(contextualizeCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(contextualizeOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.configure(extra=...)</code> sets the default <code>"-"</code>. Without it, the format's <code>{"{extra[request_id]}"}</code> raises a <code>KeyError</code> for records logged outside the block, and Loguru prints a logging error to stderr in place of the line.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="exceptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · Errors</p>
                    <h2>Exceptions and multiple processes</h2>

                    <h3 id="catch" class="scroll-mt-24">logger.catch and logger.exception</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>@logger.catch</code> logs any exception the function raises at <code>ERROR</code> with its traceback, then swallows it. Use it on the entry point of a script or a worker thread, where an uncaught exception would otherwise vanish; <code>@logger.catch(reraise=True)</code> logs and re-raises. Inside an <code>except</code> block, <code>logger.exception()</code> logs the current exception.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(catchCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Loguru tracebacks show the value of each variable in each frame. The <code>diagnose</code> argument controls that, and it is on by default:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(diagnoseCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(diagnoseOutput, "loguru-output")} /></pre>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Turn diagnose off in production</p>
                        <p>
                            The values that make the traceback useful on your machine are request bodies, tokens and customer data in production, and the Loguru docs warn that <code>diagnose</code> can leak them. Add production sinks with <code>diagnose=False</code>, or start the process with <code>LOGURU_DIAGNOSE=NO</code> for the default sink.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(productionSinkCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="multiprocessing" class="scroll-mt-24">Several processes with enqueue=True</h3>
                    <p>
                        Sinks are thread-safe without any option. <code>enqueue=True</code> puts a multiprocessing queue in front of the sink, with a background thread that drains it. Log calls stop waiting for disk or network writes, and several processes can write to one file without interleaving lines.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Child processes need the configured logger. With the <code>spawn</code> start method, the default on macOS and Windows, a child imports your module again and gets a fresh default logger, so pass the parent's logger to each worker:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(enqueueCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>context=</code> builds the sink's queue with the pool's start method. Without it, the queue uses the interpreter's default, and on Linux, where Python 3.12 defaults to <code>fork</code>, the <code>spawn</code> pool fails with <code>RuntimeError: A SemLock created in a fork context is being shared with a process in a spawn context</code>. <code>logger.complete()</code> waits until the queue is empty; call it before the process exits or the last records are lost.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="stdlib" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · Standard library</p>
                    <h2>Loguru and the logging module</h2>

                    <h3 id="intercept" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="sys-dot" style={`--sys: ${systems.stdlib.color}`}></span>Intercept standard logging</h3>
                    <p>
                        Libraries such as requests, SQLAlchemy, botocore and uvicorn log through the standard <code>logging</code> module and never call Loguru. An <code>InterceptHandler</code> on the root logger, the logger every other <code>logging</code> logger passes its records up to, re-logs each record through Loguru so one set of sinks writes everything:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(interceptCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>level=0</code> lets every record through to Loguru, where the sink levels decide what is written. <code>force=True</code> removes handlers that something else already put on the root logger. The <code>depth</code> loop makes Loguru report the line that logged, not a line inside <code>logging/__init__.py</code>. Import the module once, at startup:
                    </p>
                    <LanguageTabs tabs={interceptTabs.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))} label="Application type">
                        {interceptTabs.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <p class="client-install"><span>Run</span><code>{t.run}</code></p>
                                <pre><code set:html={highlight(t.code, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                                <p class="tab-note">{t.note}</p>
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>

                    <h3 id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru vs the logging module</h3>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.loguru.color}`}>Loguru</span></th>
                                    <th><span class="sys-badge" style={`--sys: ${systems.stdlib.color}`}>logging</span></th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {comparison.map(([task, loguru, stdlib]) => (
                                    <tr><td>{task}</td><td set:html={loguru} /><td set:html={stdlib} /></tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        For an application, Loguru replaces most of the setup code. For a library, use <code>logging</code>, so the application decides where records go.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="opentelemetry" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>06</span> · OpenTelemetry</p>
                    <h2>Send Loguru logs to OpenTelemetry</h2>
                    <p>
                        The OpenTelemetry Python SDK receives logs through <code>LoggingHandler</code>, a handler for the standard <code>logging</code> module. When a record is logged inside an active <strong>span</strong>, the unit of work a trace is made of, the handler adds the span's trace ID and span ID, which is what links a log line to its request. Loguru never calls <code>logging</code> handlers, so its records reach OpenTelemetry only through a sink that hands them over:
                    </p>

