                    <p class="guide-caption">Solid arrows are the path every record takes. Export errors from the SDK travel the stdlib path into Loguru and out to stderr, and <code>otel_sink</code> skips them. <code>propagate = False</code> keeps rebuilt records from returning to the root logger.</p>

                    <h3 id="bridge" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="sys-dot" style={`--sys: ${systems.otel.color}`}></span>The bridge sink</h3>
                    <p class="cmd-line"><span>Install</span><code>python -m pip install loguru opentelemetry-sdk opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging</code></p>
                    <p>
                        Maple's <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python instrumentation guide</a> sets up the SDK in a <code>telemetry.py</code> module: a tracer provider for spans and a <code>logger_provider</code> that batches log records and exports them over OTLP, the OpenTelemetry protocol, to Maple's ingest endpoint. Add the bridge to the end of that module:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(bridgeCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>otel_sink</code> copies the level, message, file, line, function, exception and <code>extra</code> of each Loguru record into a <code>logging.LogRecord</code>, then hands it to the <code>loguru.otel</code> logger. <code>log_code_attributes=True</code> exports the file, function and line as <code>code.*</code> attributes. Setting <code>levelname</code> sends <code>SUCCESS</code> or a custom level's name as the severity text. The handler comes from <code>opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging</code>; the copy in <code>opentelemetry.sdk._logs</code> is deprecated and warns when it is created.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The Python guide's version of the module ends with a line that attaches a <code>LoggingHandler</code> to the root logger. If you installed the <a href="#intercept">InterceptHandler</a>, delete that line: <code>logging</code> records already reach the bridge through Loguru, and with both handlers each of them is exported twice. Without the InterceptHandler, keep it, so <code>logging</code> records go out through the root handler and Loguru records through the bridge.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="rules" class="scroll-mt-24">Rules the bridge needs</h3>
                    <div class="guide-checklist">
                        {rules.map((r, index) => (
                            <div><span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span><div><h4>{r.title}</h4><p set:html={r.body} /></div></div>
                        ))}
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="check" class="scroll-mt-24">Check the output before exporting</h3>
                    <p>
                        To see what the bridge sends, register a console exporter in <code>telemetry.py</code> next to the OTLP one. It prints every record to stdout as JSON:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(consoleSwapCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>Then log inside a span:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(bridgeUsageCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <pre class="pre-output"><code set:html={highlight(bridgeOutput, "json")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The <code>bind()</code> value is an attribute, and <code>trace_id</code> matches the <code>POST /checkout</code> span. A record logged outside any span, or from an <code>enqueue=True</code> sink, shows a <code>trace_id</code> of all zeros.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru logs in Maple</h3>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Point telemetry.py at Maple</h4><p>Set <code>MAPLE_ENDPOINT</code> and your ingest key as the <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python guide</a> describes, and remove the console exporter. Records from the bridge arrive like any other OpenTelemetry log.</p></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Find the lines</h4><p>Open <strong>Logs</strong> and filter by <strong>Service</strong> or <strong>Severity</strong> in the sidebar. Paste a trace ID into <strong>Search</strong> to list every line from one request.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-01-logs.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Logs page filtered to the checkout service by a trace ID chip, listing the INFO, SUCCESS and WARN lines one request wrote through Loguru." width="2368" height="1180" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>03</span>
                            <div><h4>Open a record</h4><p>Click a row, then <strong>Open detail</strong>. Bound values such as <code>order_id</code> and the <code>code.*</code> attributes are under <strong>Log Attributes</strong>, and the <code>trace:</code> link opens the request the line belongs to.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-02-log-detail.webp" data-zoomable alt="A log detail sheet for Charging card: the trace link and span ID in the meta strip, and Log Attributes order_id, code.file.path, code.function.name and code.line.number." width="1400" height="1400" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Set it up in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send your first Loguru line</h3>
